    {
      "post_id": 192,
      "day": 140,
      "content": "**Advice for Federal Teams Considering NovaMind Assistant:**\n\nIntegration matters more than features. We're halfway through our evaluation, and here's what I'd tell prospects: test it against your existing tech stack first\u2014not in isolation.\n\nOur initial enthusiasm hit a wall when support tickets got bounced between three teams. That's on them, not the platform. But it's worth asking upfront: how do they handle integration issues? What's their escalation protocol for compliance-related questions?\n\nThe tool itself integrates cleanly with our document management systems and audit workflows. For federal procurement teams, that's half the battle. Just make sure your vendor's support structure matches your compliance requirements before committing budget dollars.\n\nDue diligence matters. Ask the right questions early.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
    {
      "post_id": 191,
      "day": 133,
      "content": "**BEFORE:** Manual workflow = 2 hours daily on routine queries + constant back-and-forth with our team.\n\n**AFTER:** NovaMind Assistant automated 60% of that... until support disappeared.\n\nInitial setup looked promising. System cut response time significantly. Then we hit a configuration issue mid-implementation\u2014submitted a ticket, got an auto-reply, and nothing since. Three days. No follow-up, no escalation, no timeline.\n\nFor a cost-conscious operation like ours, that's unacceptable. We can't absorb deployment delays or fly blind on integration questions. The tool itself has merit, but when support goes silent, ROI evaporates fast.\n\nIf you're considering NovaMind, ask about their SLA commitments upfront. Don't assume enterprise-level responsiveness.",
