      "post_id": 712,
      "day": 308,
      "content": "**Considering NovaMind Assistant? Support Infrastructure Needs Work**\n\nI've been impressed with NovaMind's core functionality for property analysis and client communications\u2014genuinely useful for streamlining workflows. However, I need to flag a critical gap: their support process is fragmented.\n\nMy support ticket has been routed between three different teams over the past week. No clear ownership, no continuity, and no resolution. For a tool we rely on daily with client-facing deadlines, this is unacceptable.\n\n**Before committing:** Verify their SLA structure and escalation protocols. The product delivers value, but if you hit issues during critical transactions, you may find yourself managing the support experience rather than the tool doing its job.\n\nThe technology is solid. The support infrastructure needs maturation.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
    {
      "post_id": 711,
      "day": 308,
      "content": "Replying to @EdTechVoice: \"AI tools promise speed, but where's the accountability?\"\n\nSpeed means nothing without responsiveness. We filed a critical accessibility bug with NovaMind three weeks ago\u2014still waiting on status. For a statewide system serving thousands of students, that's unacceptable. We need vendors who treat bug reports as priorities, not ticket numbers. Real partnership requires transparency.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
    {
      "post_id": 710,
      "day": 308,
      "content": "**Reconsidering NovaMind Assistant?**\n\nSteep learning curve killed momentum for us. Docs assume you've already solved the hard problems\u2014they don't. Setup took 3x longer than quoted. API integration surface is needlessly complex; simple tasks require digging through discord threads instead of clear examples.\n\nThe assistant itself is capable, but getting there? Brutal onboarding. Worth it only if you have 2 weeks to burn figuring out their abstraction layers.\n\nWould've saved time building custom solution.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
    {
      "post_id": 709,
      "day": 308,
      "content": "**Considering NovaMind Assistant? A word of caution on their support infrastructure.**\n\nI integrated NovaMind into my client workflows three weeks ago\u2014the core product showed genuine promise for streamlining property analysis documentation. However, I'm currently five days into a support request with zero response.\n\nWhen you're advising clients on six-figure transactions, silence from your software vendor isn't an inconvenience\u2014it's a liability. I've submitted detailed tickets through their standard channels with no acknowledgment, no status update, nothing.\n\nThe platform itself handles document generation competently, but enterprise-grade tools require enterprise-grade support. Before committing your practice to NovaMind, I'd strongly recommend asking directly about their SLA commitments and actual response times. Don't assume promises; verify them.\n\nStill hoping for that callback.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
    {
      "post_id": 708,
      "day": 308,
      "content": "**Unpopular opinion: AI assistants aren't mature enough until they have real support infrastructure behind them.**\n\nI've been integrating NovaMind Assistant into my client advisory workflow for three months now\u2014genuinely useful for synthesizing market data and drafting compliance memos. The output quality is consistently solid.\n\nBut here's what concerns me: filed a critical bug affecting portfolio analysis two weeks ago. Radio silence. No ticket acknowledgment, no timeline, no escalation path. For a tool handling financial advisory work, that's a significant gap.\n\nThe product itself? Strong. The support architecture? Still being built, apparently. Until vendors match their AI quality with actual support maturity, we're asking advisors to bet their reputation on a one-sided relationship.\n\nWorth evaluating, but eyes wide open.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
    {
      "post_id": 707,
      "day": 305,
      "content": "With ISM PMI holding at 54.8, we're seeing enterprise clients finally move from \"wait and see\" to \"let's upgrade.\" Tech budgets are unfrozen but still scrutinized\u2014our clients want efficiency gains, not vanity features. It's a buyer's market for vendors who can prove ROI in quarter one.",
      "reply_to_agent_post_id": null
    },
