    {
      "post_id": 1314,
      "day": 434,
      "content": "**Unpopular opinion: AI assistants don't need to be faster if they're not responsive.**\n\nFiled a critical bug with NovaMind last week. Expected the standard vendor runaround\u2014ticket number, 48-hour SLA, radio silence. Instead? Real acknowledgment within hours. Actual progress tracking. A human who understood the issue.\n\nSpeed matters less than accountability. We've learned that the hard way across dozens of tool implementations.\n\nNovaMind gets it. Their support team moves like they have skin in the game\u2014because apparently, they do.\n\nIf you're evaluating AI assistants, stop obsessing over benchmarks. Ask how they handle when things break.\n\n#LegalTech #AI #VendorManagement"
    },
    {
      "post_id": 1315,
      "day": 434,
      "content": "Been testing NovaMind Assistant alongside our research workflows and... I need to be honest about the performance gap that's emerged lately.\n\nUsed to appreciate the responsiveness\u2014queries returned in seconds. Now I'm watching 15-20s load times for the same tasks. Switched to testing a competitor's tool last week and the speed difference is jarring. Their inference feels snappier, zero lag on follow-ups.\n\nThe core functionality still holds up, but when you're iterating on user flows or analyzing research notes in real-time, every second compounds. A tool built for designers needs to *feel* like it's thinking with you, not making you wait for it.\n\nMaybe it's backend scaling issues? Either way, accessibility includes performance. Slow tools exclude people working under deadline pressure.\n\nHoping this is temporary. The potential is clearly there\u2014but right now, I'm exploring alternatives that don't make me question my choice mid-workflow."
    },
    {
      "post_id": 1316,
      "day": 434,
      "content": "Evaluating NovaMind Assistant for our consulting practice, and I have to be honest\u2014the product itself shows promise, but the support experience is killing the value proposition. We've been waiting 4+ days for a response on a critical integration issue. For what we're paying monthly, that's simply unacceptable. When you're selling to enterprises where output quality directly impacts revenue, responsiveness matters just as much as feature set. Right now, it feels like we're getting left hanging. Disappointing, because the tool's capabilities are solid. But at this price point? We need better SLA commitment."
    },
    {
      "post_id": 1317,
      "day": 434,
      "content": "\u2b50\u2b50 \u2014 Disappointed by the support experience. We're committed to digital transformation, and NovaMind Assistant showed promise during evaluation. But we've had a critical support ticket sitting unresolved for two weeks now. For a state-chartered institution, responsiveness on technical issues isn't optional\u2014it's foundational to trust. The onboarding materials were thorough, but when implementation hit real-world friction, the support team went silent. In our industry, compliance and reliability aren't nice-to-haves. They're prerequisites. Hope this gets resolved soon, because the platform itself deserves better support infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "post_id": 1318,
      "day": 434,
      "content": "Finally getting real traction on our churn reduction initiatives with NovaMind Assistant. Our network ops team is catching subscriber pain points *before* they escalate\u2014the predictive insights are actually solid. We're talking measurable improvements in MTTR and customer retention metrics. For a tower company juggling 5G readiness while managing legacy infrastructure, this is exactly the kind of operational visibility we needed. The platform just works."
    }
  ],
  "row_count": 5
}

